; =============================================================================
; 2. PROJECT PHILOSOPHY / DESIGN GUARDRAILS
; =============================================================================
;
; Casual Run is an SNES-style improvement patch, not a remake. Its goals are:
;   - bug fixes, QoL, and gentle rebalance toward a faster casual playthrough;
;   - less grinding and fewer softlocks / missable traps;
;   - optional depth for experienced players without forcing optimization;
;   - translation/plot inconsistencies may be treated as bugs;
;   - vanilla preservation is not a goal when friction can be reduced cleanly;
;   - preserve the SNES presentation language: no boss-HP overlays, numeric combat
;     overlays, modern HUD clutter, or fourth-wall tutorial tone;
;   - minimize unnecessary menu trips and repeated/slow presentation;
;   - keep character reactions sincere; humor should come from voice/situation rather
;     than turning every speaker into a quip machine.
;
; SOURCE-CONTROL PHILOSOPHY
;   clean verified ROM -> authoritative ASM -> complete test ROM -> derived release IPS
;   The source must explain native hooks, expected old behavior/bytes where known,
;   expansion ownership, WRAM dependencies, and subsystem rationale.
;
; =============================================================================
; 3. SOURCE-RECOVERY CONTRACT AND EVIDENCE RULES
; =============================================================================
;
; ORIGIN
;   Casual Run began as layered binary patches, including inherited work from FuSoYa,
;   Ninakoru, assassin17, and later project-specific changes. Complete original symbolic
;   source does not exist for every inherited mutation.
;
; RECOVERY BASIS
;   The v1.03 authoritative baseline was reconstructed from:
;     - the exact clean U.S. ROM;
;     - the cumulative v1.03 IPS;
;     - archived cumulative IPS milestones and release notes.
;
; RECOVERY METHOD
;   1. Apply every archived cumulative milestone to the same clean ROM.
;   2. Compare successive outputs.
;   3. For every byte surviving into v1.03, record the most recent milestone that
;      changed it.
;   4. Compare final v1.03 against clean ROM.
;   5. Merge nearby mutations only within the same 64 KiB HiROM bank; intervening bytes
;      are copied exactly from v1.03.
;   6. Emit the result in physical CPU-address order.
;   7. Rebuild and require the exact v1.03 SHA-256.
;
; EVIDENCE RULE
;   Understood behavior is named and documented. Behavior that is not fully understood
;   remains exact recovered db data. Do NOT replace exact binary evidence with a guessed
;   symbolic explanation. Converting an opaque block to readable code is accepted only
;   when the rebuilt ROM remains identical, or when a difference is a deliberate new
;   feature with its own validation.
;
; PROVENANCE RULE
;   A block's 'last surviving mutation provenance' identifies the latest archived build
;   known to have changed those surviving bytes. It is not automatically complete
;   semantic ownership; one release may contain multiple features and later releases may
;   keep an earlier helper while modifying its caller.
;
; PHYSICAL SOURCE BODY
;   The recovered v1.03 body contains 266 named mutation blocks. Later v1.04-v1.06
;   source-authored hooks/data are appended at their actual CPU locations. The full file
;   currently contains 3,307 org directives. Existing recovered payloads are not relocated
;   merely for aesthetic neatness.
;
